      "evidence": "**Evidence for High Risk or Moderate Risk:**\n- **About 900 state and metro series were cut.** With the January 2026 data, the state and metro program discontinued roughly 900 detailed-industry employment, hours, and earnings series. BLS said these \"will no longer be available in the BLS database at that time,\" which means past values were removed too, not just future updates. All-industry totals for states and metro areas are still published. ([SAE notice, 3/5/2026](https://www.bls.gov/sae/notices/2026/notice-of-publication-changes-with-the-release-of-january-2026-data.htm))\n- **Some national series were cut in 2025.** The annual sample review in February 2025 discontinued or merged some national industry series. Some new ones were also added. This kind of review is routine every year. ([CES notice](https://www.bls.gov/ces/notices/2025/2025-bmk-series-changes.htm))\n- **The CES Highlights report was discontinued in 2025.** Past issues (August 2004 to August 2025) are still archived online. ([notice](https://www.bls.gov/ces/notices/2025/ces-highlights-have-been-discontinued.htm))\n- **Past data were revised by design.** Benchmark revisions rewrote seasonally adjusted data back to January 2021 at both the national and state levels. This is normal practice, but it means earlier versions disappear from the BLS database.\n\n**Evidence for No Known Issue:**\n- National headline series such as total nonfarm (back to 1939), the bulk download files, and the API are all intact.\n- Old releases are mirrored on FRED, ALFRED, and FRASER.\n\n**Rating: Moderate Risk.** What was removed are data elements (detailed series) inside a dataset that otherwise survives, not whole data files. That fits the Moderate definition. A case could be made for High, since past values for those 900 series were taken out of the database.\n\n---"
