      "evidence": "**Evidence for High Risk:**\n- **Less detail:** the roughly 900 state and metro series discontinued in 2026, because of \"low or declining employment or sample coverage.\"\n- **Smaller sample:** about 122,000 businesses and agencies (666,000 worksites) on older BLS pages, then 121,000 (631,000) in the FY2027 budget, then 119,000 (622,000) on the current homepage. The budget also lists about 430 metro areas, while the homepage says about 450.\n- **Expected further losses:** the FY2027 budget expects performance targets to reflect a \"loss of series that no longer meet minimum publication standards\" and \"impacts of reduced staffing.\" It isn't clear how much of this applies to CES specifically. ([DOL FY2027 budget justification](https://www.dol.gov/sites/dolgov/files/general/budget/2027/CBJ-2027-V3-01.pdf))\n- **Fewer responses:** the response rate fell to about 43% in 2024, down from about 58% before 2020. There were record-sized benchmark revisions: \u2212911,000 preliminary and \u2212898,000 final for March 2025.\n- **Related cut:** in July 2026 BLS stopped publishing monthly state-level job openings data (JOLTS) and moved to annual releases. That shows the agency is willing to cut how often it publishes state data.\n\n**Mitigating:**\n- National monthly frequency and timeliness are unchanged.\n- The birth\u2013death model was improved in 2025\u201326 to use current sample data.\n\n**Rating: High Risk.** Sub-national detail has measurably shrunk, and the sample keeps getting smaller.\n\n---"
