      "evidence": "**Evidence for High Risk:**\n- **Commissioner fired over CES data.** The President fired the Commissioner over CES revisions, alleging manipulation without evidence. This had no precedent.\n- **Inspector General investigation.** The DOL Inspector General opened a review of BLS data collection practices in September 2025.\n- **Proposed move to Commerce.** Two budgets in a row (FY2026 and FY2027) propose moving BLS into the Department of Commerce together with Census and BEA.\n- **Cuts tied to staffing reductions.** The discontinued products and series (the Highlights report, about 900 state and metro series) followed the administration's workforce reductions.\n\n**Evidence for Moderate Risk:**\n- **Suspension idea dropped.** Antoni proposed suspending the monthly jobs report in August 2025 but later backed off, and his nomination was withdrawn. ([CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2025/08/12/economy/antoni-bls-mulls-suspending-jobs-report))\n- **Reorganization still only proposed.** The move to Commerce has not been enacted.\n- **No targeted directive.** No presidential order or Presidential Action-driven clearance targets CES content, and no negative policy note appears on the CES site.\n- **New Commissioner's pledges.** Matsumoto pledged that method changes would be science-driven and approved by career staff.\n\n**Rating: High Risk, borderline Moderate.** Firing the Commissioner directly over this dataset, the Inspector General review, and the repeated reorganization proposal are significant actions in line with administration priorities. However, no official policy has yet changed what CES collects. If Matsumoto's leadership stays stable and the reorganization stalls, a rating of Moderate would be reasonable.\n\n---"
