      "evidence": "**Evidence for No Known Issue**\n- Legal authority is named explicitly: 13 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 8(b), 131 and 193 (the economic census) and 49 U.S.C. \u00a7 6302, as cited in the 2026 notice. Businesses must respond under 13 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 224\u2013225.\n- \u00a7 6302 tells BTS to compile data on \"the volumes and patterns of movement of goods \u2026 by all modes.\" That spells out what has to be collected.\n- No substitute exists. The 2026 notice calls the CFS \"the only comprehensive and publicly available source of multimodal, system-wide data\" on goods movement.\n- Federal programs depend on it. It is the benchmark for the Freight Analysis Framework, which feeds national freight planning and the state freight plans required to receive National Highway Freight Program funds (FHWA guidance, February 24, 2026).\n\n**Evidence for Moderate Risk**\n- No law names the CFS by title or requires it to run every five years. It depends on general economic-census and BTS authority.\n- Census's FY2026 budget says it will \"eliminate functions that are not statutorily mandated or core to the Bureau's mission.\" That could be applied to a survey that isn't named in law.\n\n**Rating: No Known Issue.** The authority is explicit, the required content is clear, nothing can substitute for it, and federal programs rely on it.\n\n---"
