      "evidence": "**Evidence for No Known Issue**\n- The 2022 cycle released all its data products, finishing by March 2026.\n- Census's September 2026 notice on mandatory business surveys says: \"The next Economic Census will occur in 2028 for the reference year 2027\" ([91 FR 56835](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/09/04/2026-18176/current-mandatory-business-surveys)).\n- The Commodity Flow Survey, which is part of the 2027 census, began its OMB form approval in May 2026 ([91 FR 29449](https://thefederalregister.org/documents/2026-10118/agency-information-collection-activities-submission-to-the-office-of-management-and-budget-omb-for-review-and-approval-c)).\n- The administration's FY2027 budget asks for $166.9M for the Economic Census, $33M more than the prior level.\n\n**Evidence for Moderate or High Risk**\n- **The form approval has lapsed.** The approval for the 2022 Economic Census (OMB 0607-0998) expired on June 30, 2025, more than a year ago ([reginfo.gov](https://www.reginfo.gov/public/do/PRAViewICR?ref_nbr=202202-0607-010)).\n  - This is normal for the census. The approval is renewed before each cycle, and in 2022 Census reinstated it after the 2017 approval had lapsed.\n  - Even so, a literal reading of the rubric (\"expired for more than one year\") points to High.\n- **No 2027 notice yet.** In the 2022 cycle, the 60-day comment notice appeared in August 2021, about 14 months before collection began. I didn't find the equivalent 2027 notice for the main census, which may mean it's running late. I couldn't confirm this.\n- **Shutdowns and stopgap funding.**\n  - The October 1 to November 12, 2025 shutdown halted Census economic work.\n  - A stopgap funding bill (continuing resolution, or CR) is likely for FY2027. More than 60 groups asked Commerce to request extra spending room for 2027 Economic Census preparation ([letter](https://thecensusproject.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Census-Project-Final-Letter-to-Lutnick-Re-Funding-Anomaly7-29-26.pdf)). An early draft of the President's list of such exceptions reportedly left out the Census Bureau.\n  - The Senate's stopgap bill gives Census flexibility, but only for 2030 census preparation, not the Economic Census. The House version gave Census nothing ([Census Project, Aug. 2026](https://thecensusproject.org/2026/08/31/august-2026-census-project-update/)).\n- **Possible delays from the new confidentiality order.** It requires a legal review of each product before release. The Data Foundation notes this \"adds a step\" that could slow releases ([Data Foundation](https://datafoundation.org/news/data-policy/886/886-DAO-216-26-Fact-Sheet)).\n\n**Rating: Moderate Risk.** Collection hasn't been skipped and no legal deadline has been missed. The lapsed approval follows the usual pattern between cycles, but the 2027 approval and funding are not yet secured. If the main 2027 form approval hasn't been started by early 2027, this rating should move to High.\n\n---"
