      "evidence": "**Evidence for \"Gone\"**\n- The portal (hifld-geoplatform.hub.arcgis.com / opendata.arcgis.com) and its ArcGIS REST services are offline. The HIFLD website was removed September 16, 2025 (NAPSG notice).\n- DHS's \"crosswalk\" spreadsheet, which linked each layer to its original publisher, was itself taken down in September 2025 (August 2026 FGDC/NGAC presentation, \"From HIFLD Open to HIFLD Next\").\n- NGA (the program's earlier sponsor) removed its own legacy HIFLD services in 2022. No federal copy of the older data remains public.\n- The DHS page (dhs.gov/gmo/hifld, last updated September 4, 2025) now describes only HIFLD Secure.\n\n**Evidence for \"High Risk\" (partial survival)**\n- Some layers still exist at the agencies that made them, such as USGS, FEMA and EPA. However, an unverified March 2026 audit by the civic-tech site My Community Data found only about 52 of roughly 365 former service URLs still working.\n- Some layers moved only to HIFLD Secure, which the public can't use.\n\n**What still exists outside the primary location**\n- DataLumos (run by ICPSR): 415 layers, DOI 10.3886/E241367V1.\n- Source Cooperative (SeerAI).\n- HIFLD Next (Public Environmental Data Partners).\n- Homeland Security Digital Library at the Naval Postgraduate School, which is DHS-funded. Its page says it preserved \"an aggregation of those open data sources.\" I could not open the page itself (403 error).\n\n**Rating: Gone.** Nothing is publicly available at the primary federal location. Copies survive only through third parties.\n\n---"
