      "evidence": "**Evidence for Moderate Risk**\n- **Timeliness:** it fell for several months in 2025\u201326 and has since been restored.\n- **Response rate:** MARTS is voluntary, and the larger survey it draws from has a response rate of only about 63.7% for sales ([2026 revision introduction](https://www.census.gov/retail/mrts/www/benchmark/2026/pdf/Introduction.pdf)).\n- **Smaller sample:** it was cut to about 4,800 firms in 2022 (before 2025).\n- **Narrower coverage:** nonemployers were excluded starting April 2025.\n- **Paper forms:** paper collection is being phased out starting late 2025 (90 FR 37468).\n- **Strain on sister programs:** Census froze revisions to a sister survey's figures for 2026 \"due to schedule and resource constraints\" ([MTIS Jan 2026](https://www2.census.gov/mtis/historical/mtis2601.pdf)). This is not MARTS, but it suggests strain in the same economic directorate.\n- **Field staff losses:** the Commerce Inspector General has reported shortages of survey field staff, and reporters have described losses in collection staff ([GovExec](https://www.govexec.com/workforce/2025/07/census-has-long-struggled-staffing-shortages-employees-say-trump-making-it-worse/406840/)).\n\n**Evidence for No Known Issue**\n- Industry detail, monthly frequency, error ranges and the 90% confidence intervals are all unchanged.\n- The release lag has returned to its normal ~2 weeks.\n\n**Assessment: Moderate Risk.** There has been no lasting cut in detail, frequency or timeliness. Risks are emerging from staffing, budget and response rates, and timeliness has been disrupted temporarily."
