      "evidence": "**Evidence for High or Moderate Risk**\n- **Permissive authority:** Title 13 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7131 and 182 allow Census to run surveys but do not require MARTS by name or say what it must collect. Responses are voluntary.\n- **Partial substitutes:** the full Monthly Retail Trade Survey (MRTS) gives the same figures about a month later. Private card-spending data (e.g., Visa SpendingPulse, Bank of America, the Chicago Fed's CARTS) cover part of the same ground.\n- **\"Non-mandated functions\" language:** the FY2026 budget justification said Census would eliminate functions \"not statutorily mandated or core to the Bureau's mission\" ([Census Project](https://thecensusproject.org/2025/06/30/department-of-commerce-releases-fiscal-year-2026-census-budget-details/)).\n\n**Evidence for No Known Issue or Moderate Risk**\n- **Federal program use:** BEA uses it for GDP and the national accounts, as stated in the ICR abstract. The Federal Reserve, BLS and Treasury also use it.\n- **Indicator status:** it is an OMB-designated Principal Federal Economic Indicator under Statistical Policy Directives 3 and 4.\n- **No full substitute:** no other official, probability-based measure of spending is available at a two-week lag.\n\n**Assessment: Moderate Risk.** No statute names MARTS specifically. GDP estimation and monetary policy rely on it, and the substitutes are only partial: MRTS is a month later and private data are not representative. The general authority and the \"non-mandated\" language keep this from being No Known Issue."
